    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu I</a> united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> chiefdoms in Ghana's forest zone around 1700, bound together by the sacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>.</li><li>Rich in gold and kola, Asante resisted British expansion through five wars between 1824 and 1900.</li><li>Annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1902, it never surrendered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>, and its king is still recognised in Ghana today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>No one sits on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-stool">Golden Stool</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> is enstooled beneath it, as it embodies the soul of the whole Asante nation.</p></aside>
    </section>

